e":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01HyLLEnW9AHWhMgoP8zhppt","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"      highEnchantmentSurcharge(items) +\n      firstInsuranceSurcharge -\n      loyaltyDiscount -\n      followUpDiscount +\n      PROCESSING_FEE,\n  );\n}\n\n// Premiums always round up (in the MHPCO's favor).\nexport function roundPremium(amount: number): number {\n  return Math.ceil(amount);\n}\n\n// Payouts always round down (in the MHPCO's favor).\nexport function roundPayout(amount: number): number {\n  return Math.floor(amount);\n}\n\ninterface Customer {\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number;\n}\n\ninterface QuoteStep {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: Item[];\n}\n\ninterface Damage {\n  itemType: string;\n  amount: number;\n}\n\ninterface ClaimStep {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: {\n    cause: string;\n    damages: Damage[];\n  };\n}\n\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\ninterface Scenario {\n  customer: Customer;\n\n[83 more lines in file. Use offset=190 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784908763290},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The cleanest approach is to have `runScenario` check for `step.op === \"quote\"` to narrow the type. Since the input type expects `op: \"quote\"` literals but JSON.parse returns `any`, the CLI is already handling this fine. The spec passes plain object literals typed as strings though, so I could relax the input types to use `string` for the op field and narrow it inside the function instead of relying on the discriminated union.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"These are test-only type errors (external JSON input is untyped anyway). Let me make `runScenario` accept a permissive input type, which is more correct for JSON input and resolves the spec errors."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01WpCeJwWou1ffQQxncnZV39","name":"read","arguments":{"offset":190,"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":650,"output":579,"cacheRead":103044,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":104273,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784908763294,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-35847c86-4f91-4243-a28a-20880996ded9","responseModel":"claude-opus-4-8"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01WpCeJwWou1ffQQxncnZV39","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"  steps: Step[];\n}\n\ninterface QuoteResult {\n  premium: number;\n}\n\ninterface ClaimResult {\n  payout: number;\n  remainingCap: number;\n}\n\nconst DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst CAP_MULTIPLIER = 2;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_PAYOUT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_PAYOUT_RATE = 0.5;\n\ninterface Policy {\n  items: Item[];\n  remainingCap: number;\n}\n\nfunction reimbursedAmount(item: Item, damageAmount: number): number {\n  if (enchantmentAtLeast(item, HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_PAYOUT_THRESHOLD)) {\n    return damageAmount * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_PAYOUT_RATE;\n  }\n  return damageAmount;\n}\n\nfunction damagePayout(item: Item, damage: Damage): number {\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`Damage amount cannot be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n  const reimbursed = reimbursedAmount(item, damage.amount);\n  return roundPayout(Math.max(0, reimbursed - DEDUCTIBLE));\n}\n\nfunction totalDamagePayout(policy: Policy, damages: Damage[]): number {\n  const availableByType = groupByType(policy.items);\n\n  return damages.reduce((total, damage) => {\n    const item = availableByType.get(damage.itemType)?.pop();\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    return total + damagePayout(item, damage);\n  }, 0);\n}\n\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, step: ClaimStep): ClaimResult {\n  const grossPayout = totalDamagePayout(policy, step.incident.damages);\n  const payout = Math.min(grossPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): {\n  results: (QuoteResult | ClaimResult)[];\n} {\n  const { customer, steps } = scenario;\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n\n  const results = steps.map((step) => {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      validateItemTypes(step.items);\n      const premium = quotePremium(\n        step.items,\n        customer.yearsWithMHPCO,\n        quoteCount > 0,\n      );\n      quoteCount += 1;\n      policies.push({\n        items: step.items,\n        remainingCap: insuranceSum(step.items) * CAP_MULTIPLIER,\n      });\n      return { premium };\n    }\n    return processClaim(policies[step.policy], step);\n  });\n\n  return { results };\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784908773458},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The discriminated union approach works with properly typed literals, but test literals infer `op` as a generic string, breaking the type discrimination. I could loosen the parameter type to accept JSON input while keeping type safety inside the function, but changing `op` to just `string` would prevent the discriminated union from working. I'm thinking through whether to adjust the field comparison or the type definition itself.\n\nThe real issue is that if both `QuoteStep` and `ClaimStep` have `op: string`, TypeScript can't narrow between them even with a type guard. The cleanest solution is to accept a loosely-typed input at the boundary and cast it internally—define an input type that mirrors the JSON structure without strict discrimination, then narrow with a type guard inside the function. Since `JSON.parse` returns `any` anyway, the CLI doesn't care about types, and the spec type errors won't break the test runner.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"The spec type errors arise because test literals infer `op: string` rather than the literal `\"quote\"`. Since real input is untyped JSON, I'll accept a permissive input shape at the boundary and narrow internally — this is more correct and clears the errors."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01TeKjxSs7mLnEK9pg15Lbjy","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"interface QuoteStep {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: Item[];\n}\n\ninterface Damage {\n  itemType: string;\n  amount: number;\n}\n\ninterface ClaimStep {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: {\n    cause: string;\n    damages: Damage[];\n  };\n}\n\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\ninterface Scenario {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: Step[];\n}\n\n// Input arrives as untyped JSON; callers may pass loosely-typed step objects.\ntype ScenarioInput = {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: readonly ({ op: string } & Record<string, unknown>)[];\n};","oldText":"interface QuoteStep {\n  op: \"quote\";\n  items: Item[];\n}\n\ninterface Damage {\n  itemType: string;\n  amount: number;\n}\n\ninterface ClaimStep {\n  op: \"claim\";\n  policy: number;\n  incident: {\n    cause: string;\n    damages: Damage[];\n  };\n}\n\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\ninterface Scenario {\n  customer: Customer;\n  steps: Step[];\n}"}],"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":1599,"output":1059,"cacheRead":103229,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":105887,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784908773462,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-fd1d6f39-44e2-4134-abeb-97b6d0895421","responseModel":"claude-opus-4-8"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01TeKjxSs7mLnEK9pg15Lbjy","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in src/claim-office.ts."}],"details":{"diff":"     ...\n 189   customer: Customer;\n 190   steps: Step[];\n 191 }\n 192 \n+193 // Input arrives as untyped JSON; callers may pass loosely-typed step 